The income from rent, rates, taxes, repairs, and insurance in a business or profession is calculated by allowing specific deductions under Section 28 of the Income-Tax Act 2025. These deductions include:

- Premiums paid for insurance against damage or destruction of premises, machinery, plant, or furniture;  
- Land revenue, local rates, or municipal taxes paid;  
- Rent paid when premises are occupied by the assessee as a tenant;  
- Current repairs to premises (not capital expenditure) when occupied otherwise than as a tenant;  
- Current repairs to premises (not capital expenditure) when occupied as a tenant and the assessee undertakes to bear repair costs;  
- Current repairs to machinery, plant, or furniture (not capital expenditure).  

These deductions are subject to the condition that the premises, building, machinery, plant, or furniture is used for the purposes of business or profession, and if only partly used, the deduction is restricted to the fair proportionate part as determined by the Assessing Officer.  

(Source: Section 28: Rent, rates, taxes, repairs and insurance)

The cost of acquisition for units in a segregated portfolio under a mutual fund is calculated using the formula:

X = A × B / C

Where:  
- X = cost of acquisition of the unit or units in the segregated portfolio;  
- A = cost of acquisition of the unit or units in the total portfolio;  
- B = net asset value of the asset transferred to the segregated portfolio;  
- C = net asset value of the total portfolio immediately before segregation of portfolios.  

(Source: Section 73: Cost with reference to certain modes of acquisition, Sl. No. 11)

Yes, a new manufacturing domestic company in India can choose a lower tax rate on its income if it started production by March 2024, provided it meets the specified conditions.

Under **Section 201(1)** of the Income-Tax Act, 2025, such a company may opt for a tax rate of **15% on the total income other than the income mentioned in clauses (b), (c), and (d)**, if it:

- Is a domestic company engaged in the business of manufacture or production of any article or thing;  
- Has been set up and registered on or after **1st October, 2019**;  
- Has commenced manufacturing or production of an article or thing **on or before 31st March, 2024**;  
- Exercises the option in the manner provided in sub-section (2);  
- And the total income is computed as per the provisions of sub-section (3).

Thus, the lower tax rate of 15% is available under the conditions specified in **Section 201**, including the production commencement by March 2024.  

**Source: Section 201(1), Income-Tax Act 2025**.

Yes, the tax authority can revise an assessment order if it thinks it was made without proper investigation, but only under specific conditions.

According to Section 378(1)(b), the Competent Authority may, for any order passed by an authority subordinate to him, make such inquiry or cause such inquiry to be made, and pass an order thereon as he thinks fit, subject to the provisions of the Act. However, the Competent Authority shall not revise an order if:

- The order has been made more than one year previously (Section 378(2));  
- An appeal against the order lies and has not been made, or the time for appeal has not expired (Section 378(5)(a));  
- The assessee has not waived his right of appeal (Section 378(5)(b));  
- The order has been made the subject of appeal (Section 378(5)(c)).

Additionally, an application for revision must be made within one year from the date the order was communicated (Section 378(3)), and the authority may admit a late application if the assessee was prevented by sufficient cause (Section 378(4)).

Thus, while the authority can revise an order if it deems it was made without proper investigation, such revision is subject to time limits and procedural restrictions.  

Source: Section 378: Revision of other orders (Income-Tax Act 2025)
